<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peru</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peru is a country on the Pacific coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, formerly the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> conquered it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> between 1532 and 1535. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>, established in 1542, was one of the two primary administrative units of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and a major source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peru.jpg" alt="Peru" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Benbennick · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Peru.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chavín de Huántar lies at about 3,180 metres in the Conchucos valley of <b>Peru</b>’s Ancash region, east of the Cordillera Blanca, where the Huachecsa stream joins the Mosna River.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chavin">Chavín · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Morazán’s presidential term expired in February 1839 and no successor was elected; he held out in El Salvador as its head of state, but after his defeat at Guatemala City in March 1840 he left for exile in <b>Peru</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Colombian troops then carried the war into <b>Peru</b>: Bolívar won the cavalry Battle of Junín in August 1824, and Sucre destroyed the last royalist field army at the Battle of Ayacucho on 9 December 1824, ending Spanish rule in South America.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Seville held the monopoly of trade with the Americas through the Casa de Contratación, and convoys of the treasure fleets brought silver from the mines of Potosí in <b>Peru</b>, discovered in 1545, and Zacatecas in New Spain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Quechua, spread by the Incas and later used by missionaries, is today spoken by millions of people in <b>Peru</b>, Bolivia, Ecuador and neighbouring countries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Chimor grew in the Moche valley of <b>Peru</b>’s north coast, the old heartland of the Moche, after the Moche centres there were abandoned around 800.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-chimor">Kingdom of Chimor · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
